- Base case (user numbers, burn = net $72K):
  - Runway = 2,100 / 72 = 29.2 months.
  - Quarterly revenue = $1.8M; monthly revenue ≈ $600K.

- Conservative planning case (explains user 20-month runway):
  - Example assumptions to get ~20 months:
    - Increased net burn to $105K/month (2,100 / 20 = 105). That could be caused by planned hiring, capex, or lower collections.
    - OR assume revenue/cash receipts drop and net burn rises; e.g., 30% revenue decline and same opex could increase net burn.
  - I recommend we explicitly show the board the assumption that converts 29→20 months (so they see what we’re planning for).

- Downside example (stress test)
  - Revenue drop 25%, collections worsen (+15 days DSO), and temporary hires raise net burn to $150K/mo → runway = 2,100 / 150 = 14 months. Board needs to see triggers for immediate actions at this threshold.

6) KPIs to show on the board slide (recommended)
- Cash balance (current + 12-mo projected)
- Runway (months) under base / conservative / downside
- Monthly net cash burn (and components: revenue receipts, cash opex, capex)
- Quarterly revenue and YoY / QoQ growth
- Gross margin and operating margin
- DSO, inventory days, payables days (working capital cycle)
- Customer concentration (% revenue from top 5 customers)
- Sales pipeline (ARR or expected bookings next 90 days)

7) Recommended immediate actions (next 30–90 days)
- Confirm and reconcile burn definition and the 20-month assumption — update models and board pack.
- Tighten AR/Credit terms and start weekly collections focus.
- Pause non-essential hiring and discretionary spend until board approves ramp.
- Prepare a one-page contingency financing plan (preferred instrument, target amount, and timing).
- Run scenario slide with triggers (e.g., if runway < 18 months, initiate financing; if revenue down 20%, cut discretionary spend by X%).

8) Ready-to-use board slide bullets (short)
- Slide title: Financial Snapshot
  - Cash: $2.1M | Last quarter revenue: $1.8M | Net burn: $72K/mo
