I can help — below are a ready-to-use board summary, a concise finance narrative, key risks (with likelihood/impact and mitigations), suggested metrics to track, scenario ideas, and recommended board asks. Before you finalize, one quick clarification: the numbers you gave contain a discrepancy — $2.1M cash divided by a $72K/month burn implies ~29.2 months of runway (2,100,000 / 72,000 ≈ 29.17). You stated 20 months runway. Which is correct, and is your burn the net cash burn (after revenue) or operating cash burn (cash difference excluding financing)? If you confirm, I’ll adjust any wording/forecasts accordingly.

Board summary (one-paragraph executive summary)
- Metro Manufacturing finished the last quarter with revenue of $1.8M and cash of $2.1M. Current monthly cash burn is $72K, which—if that is the net cash burn—implies roughly 29 months of runway on current cash (please confirm the runway figure). Revenue momentum last quarter is healthy relative to historical periods, but profitability and working-capital dynamics require close monitoring. We recommend presenting a 12–24 month forecast with base, upside, and downside scenarios and asking the board to approve contingency liquidity measures (credit line, staged cost reductions, and customer-payment initiatives) to protect against supply, demand, or margin shocks.

Finance narrative (short, CEO/CFO-friendly)
- Performance: Revenue in Q[most recent] was $1.8M (≈ $600K/month), demonstrating sustained demand across our core product lines. Gross margin trends remain the priority—current revenue growth must translate to consistent positive operating cash flow.
- Liquidity: Cash on hand is $2.1M. At the stated cash burn of $72K/month, the company has substantial runway (confirming calculation). We are focused on preserving optionality: keep discretionary spending paused, accelerate receivables, and negotiate supplier terms to reduce working-capital needs.
- Outlook & actions: We propose a three-scenario forecast (base = plan, downside = -20% revenue + slower collections, upside = +15% revenue + margin improvements). Near-term actions: finalize forecast, secure at least one committed liquidity source (credit facility or bridge), and implement rapid margin initiatives to protect EBITDA.

Key risks, likelihood, impact, and suggested mitigations
1) Customer concentration
- Likelihood: Medium | Impact: High
- Why: A small number of customers could drive a large share of revenue.
- Mitigation: Diversify sales pipeline, prioritize cross-sell/up-sell, negotiate multi-year contracts, set credit limits; accelerate new customer acquisition with targeted sales incentives.

2) Receivables / working capital strain
- Likelihood: Medium | Impact: High
- Why: Delays in customer payments reduce usable cash despite revenue.
- Mitigation: Tighten credit policy, invoice early/electronically, offer early-pay discounts, deploy collections sprint, push for partial advance payments on large orders.

3) Supply-chain disruption / single-source suppliers
- Likelihood: Medium | Impact: High
- Why: Production stoppage or price spikes for key inputs could compress margins or delay deliveries.
- Mitigation: Qualify second-source suppliers, maintain safety stock for critical items, renegotiate pricing and lead times, include force-majeure clauses and KPIs with suppliers.

4) Margin compression from raw-material price volatility
- Likelihood: Medium | Impact: Medium–High
- Mitigation: Hedging where appropriate, pass-through pricing clauses, product mix focus on higher-margin items, continuous cost optimization.

5) Unexpected capital expenditures or equipment failure
- Likelihood: Low–Medium | Impact: Medium–High
- Mitigation: Preventive maintenance program, allocate contingency in forecast, secure short-term capital options (equipment leases, vendor financing).

6) Market demand slowdown
- Likelihood: Low–Medium | Impact: Medium
- Mitigation: Flexible manufacturing capacity, variable cost structure where possible, accelerate marketing for backlog, promotions targeted to high-margin SKUs.

7) Regulatory / compliance or quality failures
- Likelihood: Low | Impact: High
- Mitigation: Strengthen QA processes, third-party audits, product insurance, rapid-response recall/playbook.

Suggested mitigations prioritized (near-term)
- Confirm and lock a standby credit facility or committed line: High priority
- Collections push and DSO reduction plan: High priority (fast cash benefit)
- Expense review and hiring freeze on non-essential roles: Medium-high
- Supplier backup and safety-stock plan for top 10 inputs: Medium
- Scenario-based headcount and capex contingency triggers: Medium

KPI dashboard to include for the board (monthly)
- Cash on hand
- Runway (months) — clearly state calculation and include assumptions
- Monthly net cash burn (and EBITDA)
- Revenue (monthly and quarter-to-date)
- Gross margin % and margin by product line
- Customer concentration (% revenue top 5 customers)
- DSO (days sales outstanding)
- Inventory days and turnover
- Backlog and new orders
- Capex spend vs. budget
- Working-capital gap

Scenario framework (12–24 months)
- Base case: current bookings and cost structure; show monthly P&L, cash flow, and runway.
- Downside: -15–25% revenue, +10–20% DSO, incremental supplier price increases; show when runway would reach critical thresholds and when triggers for actions occur.
- Upside: +10–20% revenue growth, margin improvement from cost savings and pricing; shows optionality for reinvestment or M&A.
- For each scenario, include trigger points and pre-approved board actions (e.g., implement hiring freeze, draw on credit line, approve capital raise).

Sample slide / board one-page summary (bullets)
- Headline: Revenue $1.8M last quarter; cash $2.1M; monthly burn $72K; runway ≈ 29 months (confirm).
- What went well: Demand momentum; stable operations; no major production disruptions last quarter.
- Outstanding issues: Customer concentration, DSO trending, supplier single-source items.
- Key metrics: Cash, runway, DSO, gross margin.
- Proposals for board approval: 1) Approve pursuit of a $X commited line of credit; 2) Approve staged cost-control plan (savings target $Y/month); 3) Authorize management to negotiate supplier terms and pursue 2–3 new high-potential accounts.
- Ask: Board approval for contingency liquidity and endorsement of the 3-scenario plan; permission to implement the collections sprint and temporary hiring freeze.

Recommended immediate next steps (this week → 30 days)
- Confirm the runway calculation and whether burn is net of revenue or not (needed to finalize narrative).
- Produce a 12-month rolling cash forecast with weekly granularity for 90 days, monthly thereafter.
- Run the downside scenario to identify the earliest liquidity trigger date and present mitigations.
- Prepare a one-page board memo and a 3–4 slide deck focused on cash, runway, key risks, and asks.
- Begin collections sprint and supplier-term negotiations; present early results at the next board meeting.

