Executive summary (one pager)
- Cash on hand: $2.1M
- Reported monthly cash burn: $72K
- Reported runway: (stated) 20 months — please confirm; calculated runway using given cash and burn = ~29.2 months
- Last quarter revenue: $1.8M (annualized ~ $7.2M if run-rate holds)
- Quick interpretation: With current burn of $72K/mo, liquidity is strong (cash covers >2 years of burn). Revenue last quarter covers the quarterly burn multiple times (quarterly burn = 72K * 3 = 216K, so burn ≈ 12% of last quarter revenue). Main focus should be validating runway assumptions, monitoring revenue sustainability and collections, and preparing contingency plans if revenue or collections weaken.

Key numbers and calculations (show your math)
- Monthly burn: $72,000
- Runway (calculated) = cash / burn = $2,100,000 / $72,000 ≈ 29.17 months (~29.2 months)
- Quarterly burn = $72,000 * 3 = $216,000
- Quarterly burn as % of last quarter revenue = $216,000 / $1,800,000 = 12.0%
- To achieve a 20-month runway with $2.1M cash, implied monthly burn would be: $2,100,000 / 20 = $105,000/mo (i.e., current burn would need to be higher or some cash is not available)

Runway sensitivity (examples to show board)
- Base (72K/mo): ~29.2 months
- If burn rises 10% (79.2K): ~26.5 months
- If burn rises 20% (86.4K): ~24.3 months
- If burn lowered 10% (64.8K): ~32.4 months
- If burn lowered 20% (57.6K): ~36.5 months

