Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
First, one quick clarification — the numbers you gave contain an internal inconsistency I want to flag before I build the narrative:

- You said cash = $2.1M and burn = $72K/month. That implies runway = 2.1M / 72K ≈ 29.2 months (not 20 months).
- If runway really is 20 months with cash of $2.1M, the implied burn is 2.1M / 20 = $105K/month.

Which is correct: (A) burn = $72K/month (so runway ≈ 29.2 months), or (B) runway = 20 months (so burn ≈ $105K/month)? Tell me which to use. Meanwhile, below is a board-ready summary and a set of key risks + mitigations built to work with either set of numbers (I’ll note where the runway math should be updated once you confirm).

Board summary (one-page / opening slide)
- Headline: Metro Manufacturing is cash-positive with $2.1M on hand and solid near-term runway; Q[?] revenue last quarter was $1.8M (annualized ~ $7.2M). We are focused on preserving optionality while investing selectively to drive growth.
- Financial snapshot (single bullets):
  - Cash: $2.1M
  - Revenue (last quarter): $1.8M (annualized run-rate ≈ $7.2M)
  - Burn: $72K/month (if correct) → runway ≈ 29 months; if runway is 20 months, implied burn ≈ $105K/month (please confirm)
  - Key leverage: receivables, working capital, customer concentration (see risks)
- Recent performance highlights:
  - Stable revenue last quarter of $1.8M. (Add growth / QoQ data if available.)
  - Cost controls in place; operating discipline enabling extended runway.
- Near-term priorities (next 3–6 months):
  1. Validate and stress-test revenue outlook and collections.
