Got it — I can help. First, a quick note about the numbers you gave: they imply two different runway calculations. I’ll show both and then give a ready-to-send board summary, a crisp finance narrative, and a prioritized list of key risks with mitigations.

Quick math / reconciliation
- Cash: $2,100,000
- Stated burn: $72,000 / month
  - Runway = cash / burn = 2,100,000 / 72,000 = 29.2 months
- If runway is actually 20 months, implied burn = 2,100,000 / 20 = $105,000 / month
- Revenue last quarter: $1.8M → monthly ≈ $600K; annualized (simple) = $1.8M × 4 = $7.2M ARR

Please confirm whether:
- Your stated burn ($72K/mo) is net cash burn (after cash receipts) or an operating cash just-for-ops figure (i.e., excluding financing or capex).
- The 20-month figure includes committed outflows (leases, one-time spends, TSAs, etc.) or expected seasonal changes.

One-page Board Summary (ready text)
Subject: Metro Manufacturing — Financial update (current cash position, runway, and priorities)

Summary:
- Cash on hand: $2.10M
- Revenue: $1.8M last quarter (≈ $600K / month; annualized ≈ $7.2M)
- Net cash burn: $72K / month (per current reporting) → runway ≈ 29 months. If runway is 20 months, implied net burn is $105K / month — please confirm which figure is correct.
- Near-term outlook: Revenue stable but margin/working-capital pressure from [insert any known driver]. Primary near-term focus: preserve cash optionality while maintaining growth channels.

Recent performance drivers:
- Top-line: [insert top customer trends, product lines with growth or decline — I can fill if you share]
- Cost base: Fixed cost run-rate vs. variable costs; any one-off items this quarter (bonuses, capex, restructuring)

Immediate priorities (next 90 days)
1. Reconcile and finalize a 12-month cash flow forecast (weekly cadence until next board meeting).
