- Customer concentration (top 5 customers % of revenue)
- Capex & covenant status

3) Top 8 key risks (prioritized) + suggested mitigations

a. Liquidity / Runway misstatement
- Risk: The stated runway may be inconsistent with actual net cash flow assumptions.
- Impact: Understated burn could force rapid cost cuts or emergency financing.
- Mitigation: Reconcile cash-flow model this week; produce a 13-week cash forecast; immediate freeze on non-essential capex until forecast validated.

b. Revenue concentration
- Risk: A small number of customers make up a large share of revenue.
- Impact: Loss of one customer would materially reduce inflows.
- Mitigation: Identify top customers, build retention plans, diversify pipeline, negotiate deposit/advance terms for large customers.

c. Working capital strain (AR and inventory)
- Risk: High DSO or stuck inventory ties up cash despite reported revenue.
- Impact: Operational cash shortfalls even with healthy topline.
- Mitigation: Tighten collections (discounts for early pay), extend supplier terms, implement inventory liquidation or consignment where possible.

d. Margin compression / rising input costs
- Risk: Raw material or freight inflation reduces gross margin.
- Impact: Higher effective burn, lower cash generation.
- Mitigation: Pass-through pricing where feasible, renegotiate supplier contracts, hedging or alternate suppliers.

e. Supply chain disruptions / single-source suppliers
- Risk: Supplier failure delays production, reduces revenue.
- Mitigation: Qualify alternate suppliers, increase safety stock strategically, add contractual SLAs.

f. Execution risk on planned cost reductions or growth initiatives
- Risk: Planned actions fail to hit targets or damage customer relationships.
- Mitigation: Prioritize low-impact, high-payback measures first; staged implementation with KPIs.

g. Covenant / debt risk (if any debt)
- Risk: Hidden covenants could be triggered by one-off items.
- Mitigation: Run covenant tests, talk to lenders early, request waivers if needed.

h. Macroeconomic / demand shock
- Risk: Recession or demand slowdown reduces orders.
- Mitigation: Scenario planning, variable-cost structure adjustments, ramp down discretionary spend.

