- Why: If top 1–3 customers make up a large % of revenue, losing one is material.  
- Impact: Large single-customer loss → revenue shock.  
- Mitigation: Track share of revenue by customer, pursue cross‑selling, negotiate multi‑quarter purchase commitments, diversify pipeline.

3) Working capital & collections (AR / inventory)
- Why: High DSO or inventory build ties up cash even with good revenue.  
- Impact: Cash constrained despite healthy top-line.  
- Mitigation: Tighten credit terms, offer early payment discounts, re-negotiate supplier terms, optimize inventory turns.

4) Unexpected one-time cash outflows (capex, vendor settlements, debt covenants)
- Why: Planned or unplanned payments can materially reduce runway.  
- Impact: Could reduce runway to <12 months quickly.  
- Mitigation: Flag any known large payments; schedule/phase capex; model covenant headroom; create a 90-day cash plan.

5) Margin compression / rising input costs
- Why: Inflation, supply chain, or pricing pressure reduces gross margins.  
- Impact: Higher net burn or reduced profitability.  
- Mitigation: Hedge key inputs where possible, raise prices carefully, increase operational efficiency.

6) Hiring and growth spending overshoot
- Why: Scaling too fast increases fixed costs (payroll, facilities).  
- Impact: Permanent step-up in burn; runway reduction.  
- Mitigation: Approve hiring by stage gates; use contractors; tie hires to measurable revenue milestones.

7) Macroeconomic / market risk
- Why: Interest rate changes, recession risk, or sector slowdown.  
- Impact: Reduced demand, funding market contraction.  
- Mitigation: Scenario planning, maintain conservative cash buffer, prioritize profitable growth.

Suggested immediate actions (next 7–14 days)
