Thanks — I can help. First, one quick clarification: the numbers you gave contain an internal inconsistency that affects the runway and the narrative. I’ll explain and then give a board-ready summary, key risks with mitigations, suggested finance narrative language, KPI checklist, and recommended asks/actions.

Quick check of the math
- Cash: $2.1M
- Stated burn: $72K/month
  - If that $72K is the net cash burn (cash outflows minus cash inflows), runway = 2,100,000 / 72,000 ≈ 29.2 months.
- Stated runway: 20 months
  - If runway is actually 20 months, implied net burn = 2,100,000 / 20 = $105K/month.
- Revenue last quarter: $1.8M → average monthly revenue ≈ $600K/month.

These three facts can’t all be true simultaneously unless “burn rate” is being reported on a different basis (e.g., burn = payroll-only, or burn = operating expense before revenue is applied). Please confirm:
1) Is the $72K/month your net cash burn (cash outflow minus cash receipts)? Or is it an operating-expense line (e.g., payroll-only)?
2) Is the 20-month runway an internal, conservative projection that already assumes some planned increases in spend or reserves?
3) Is the $1.8M last-quarter revenue recurring (subscription/contracted) or one-time/variable?

I’ll proceed assuming the common definition that “runway = cash / net burn.” If you confirm a different definition, I’ll adjust the wording.

Board summary (one-page executive summary — speak-to paragraph + bullets)
- Executive paragraph (ready to read aloud):
  “As of [period end], Metro Manufacturing holds $2.1M in cash. We reported $1.8M in revenue last quarter (≈ $600K/month). On the cash-basis figures currently provided, our stated net cash burn is $72K/month which implies roughly 29 months of runway. (Note: our internal plan indicates 20 months of runway — this implies a higher effective net burn of ≈ $105K/month; we are reconciling the difference and will confirm.) Near-term priorities are to sustain revenue growth, ensure collection and margin stability, and confirm capital needs for planned investments. We are requesting the board’s endorsement on [list any funding/expense approvals you need] and will present scenario-based funding options if runway assumptions change.”
- Key bullets to include on the slide:
  - Cash: $2.1M (as of [date])
  - Revenue (last quarter): $1.8M; monthly avg ≈ $600K
  - Reported net cash burn: $72K/month (implied runway ~29 months) — reconcile with internal 20-month runway assumption
  - Trailing indicators: [insert gross margin %, DSO, major customer concentrations — if you give these I’ll fill in]
  - Immediate asks: e.g., board approval for [hiring/capex/marketing spend], authorization to begin preliminary fundraising if downside scenario < 12 months
  - Next reviews: monthly cash update; scenario update in 4 weeks

Key risks (priority, impact, mitigation)
1) Revenue concentration (priority: high; impact: high)
   - Risk: A small number of customers represent a high share of revenue.
   - Mitigation: Diversify sales pipeline; accelerate contract renewals; require minimum notice / termination terms; prioritize sales to low-risk verticals.
   - Monitoring: % revenue from top 5 customers, pipeline coverage ratio.

2) Revenue volatility / demand shock (priority: high; impact: high)
   - Risk: Demand drop reduces cash receipts and increases net burn.
   - Mitigation: Stress test revenue scenarios (−10/−25/−40%); convert variable contracts to minimum committed contracts; tighten collections.
   - Monitoring: Month-over-month revenue change; bookings vs. target.

3) Margin compression (priority: medium-high; impact: medium-high)
   - Risk: Cost inflation or pricing pressure reduces gross margin.
   - Mitigation: Re-price where contractually possible; hedge commodity inputs; optimize production mix; reduce lower-margin SKUs.
   - Monitoring: Gross margin % by product line, unit economics.

4) Cash forecasting and runway misstatement (priority: high; impact: high)
   - Risk: Using inconsistent definitions (operational burn vs. net cash burn) leads to misguided decisions.
   - Mitigation: Standardize and publish a one-page cash model (monthly cash receipts/outflows) and define “burn” consistently; board to use the standardized model.
   - Monitoring: Monthly cash statement, forecast variance vs actual.

5) Supply chain / inventory risk (priority: medium; impact: medium)
   - Risk: Delays or higher costs creating production halts or margin impact.
   - Mitigation: Dual-source critical suppliers, safety stock, negotiate lead times, pass-through pricing clauses.
   - Monitoring: Inventory days, supplier lead time, purchase price variance.

6) Working capital (receivables & payables) (priority: medium; impact: medium)
   - Risk: DSO increases and strains cash.
   - Mitigation: Shorten payment terms, invoice automation, factoring options if needed.
   - Monitoring: DSO, days payables outstanding, cash conversion cycle.

7) Talent / execution risk (priority: medium; impact: medium)
   - Risk: Losing key finance/ops/production staff slows execution.
   - Mitigation: Retention bonuses tied to milestones, succession planning, cross-training.
   - Monitoring: Key role coverage, turnover rate.

8) Macroeconomic/regulatory risks (priority: medium; impact: variable)
   - Risk: Macro slowdown, tariffs, or regulatory changes affecting customers or input costs.
   - Mitigation: Scenario planning and contingency lines, adjust pricing, maintain flexible cost base.
   - Monitoring: Customer backlogs, new regulation trackers.

Suggested mitigation asks to present to the board
- Approve a one-page standardized cash model and require monthly board cash updates.
- Approve contingency plan triggers: e.g., if runway < 12 months OR revenue drops > 20% vs plan, authorize management to begin fundraising or implement predefined cost reductions.
- Approve up to [X] for strategic investments (if applicable) conditional on milestone delivery.
- Authorize the CFO to negotiate a short-term credit facility (size to be determined) as a backup.

Finance narrative — tone and key paragraphs (ready for the board pack)
1) Opening executive summary (2–3 sentences)
   - “We finished the quarter with $2.1M cash. Revenue was $1.8M in the quarter. Based on our current net cash burn assumptions, we have runway in excess of 20 months; we are reconciling internal assumptions and will present a standardized cash forecast to the board.”
2) Performance vs plan
   - “Revenue: On plan / ahead / behind (fill in). Key drivers were [new contracts, retention, price changes].”
   - “Margins: Gross margin was X% (explain drivers).”
   - “Cash: Cash balance movements driven by [working capital change, capex, financing].”
3) Forecast & scenarios
   - Present three scenarios: Base (current plan), Downside (−20–40% revenue), Upside (+growth investments).
   - Show months of runway under each and key actions for each trigger point.
4) Key actions & asks
   - “We recommend approving [actions above], and request board feedback on fundraising thresholds and prioritization of investment vs. cost control.”
5) One-line close
   - “We are confident in the business fundamentals, but are seeking the board’s endorsement of the standardized cash model and the contingency triggers to ensure we execute proactively.”

Recommended KPIs to include on the board slide (minimum set)
- Cash balance (end of period)
- Runway (months) — show formula and assumptions (net burn = cash outflows − cash inflows)
- Net cash burn (monthly)
- Revenue (quarter and monthly trend)
- Gross margin %
- EBITDA (or operating profit/loss)
- DSO and inventory days
- Customer concentration (% revenue from top 5 customers)
- Bookings / backlog (if applicable)
- Headcount and OPEX split (fixed vs variable)
- Capex commitments and lease obligations

Scenario examples (illustrative; I can compute exact tables once you confirm burn definition)
- If net burn = $72K/month → runway ≈ 29 months
- If net burn = $105K/month (implied by 20-month runway) → runway = 20 months
- Downside example: if net burn rises to $150K/month → runway = 14 months
- Revenue shock example: if revenue declines 30% and net burn rises correspondingly, recalculate and show actions (hire freeze, pause non-critical capex, reduce discretionary spend)

Presentation / slide structure for the board
1) Title & one-line exec summary
2) Cash & runway (one table + clear assumptions)
3) Revenue & margin trend (chart)
4) Key risks & mitigations (prioritized)
5) Scenarios & triggers (base / downside / upside)
6) Asks & approvals needed

Next steps / offer
- Confirm the definitions: is the $72K net burn (post-revenue) or an expense component? Confirm whether the 20-month runway is a conservative internal assumption or derived from planned spends.
- Share any missing metrics I listed (gross margin, DSO, customer concentration, backlog, upcoming large invoices or capex) and I’ll draft a one-page board summary and a 2-slide deck (cash/runway + risks/actions) ready to present.

If you want, I’ll draft:
